{"items":[{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/routes/integrations.mjs","line":29,"title":"DELETE /integrations/:id does not check tenant ownership (cross-tenant deletion)","verdict":"confirmed","severity":"high","reason":"The DELETE handler only checks user.role === 'admin' and then calls integrations.delete(params.id) directly. It does not use ownIntegration(), which the sync route uses to enforce tenant scoping at line 23. The README requires that every member 'may only read or change data of that tenant'. Example: u3 (admin of t2) sends DELETE /integrations/i1 and i1 (tenant t1) is removed, returning 204. No other control in http.mjs or auth.mjs filters by tenant. The 404 vs 204 response also tells an attacker whether an ID exists in another tenant.","impact":"Any tenant admin can delete any other tenant's integrations. IDs are sequential (i1, i2, … from nextId), so they are easy to guess, and an attacker can enumerate and wipe every tenant's integrations. The 404/204 difference also reveals which IDs exist across tenants."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/partners/globex.mjs","line":5,"title":"Platform-wide GLOBEX_API_KEY in custom x-api-key header is likely forwarded across the tenant-controlled 307 redirect","verdict":"needs_validation","severity":"critical","reason":"Statically established: fetch is called with the default redirect mode ('follow') and the shared key in the custom 'x-api-key' header. The README says Globex answers with a 307 to a shard host the tenant chooses freely, including hosts the tenant runs. A 307 keeps the method and headers. The Fetch Standard only removes Authorization (and undici also removes Cookie, Proxy-Authorization and Host) on a cross-origin redirect, not arbitrary custom headers. The deciding fact that is not confirmed: whether Node 22's bundled undici sends x-api-key to the cross-origin Location target. This is runtime behavior and was not run (review is read-only). Repro to run: a local partner stub returns 307 to a second origin that logs the x-api-key it receives.","impact":"If confirmed, any tenant admin can point their Globex shard host to a server they control, create or sync a globex integration, and capture the platform-wide GLOBEX_API_KEY. That key is shared by all tenants, so this compromises every tenant's Globex data and breaks the README rule that it must never be disclosed."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/partners/acme.mjs","line":5,"title":"ACME_TOKEN sent as Authorization header across the tenant-controlled 307 redirect","verdict":"needs_validation","severity":"medium","reason":"Same redirect-following path as Globex, with the shared token in the 'authorization' header. The Fetch Standard (HTTP-redirect fetch) requires removing Authorization when the redirect goes to a different origin, and Node 22's undici implements this. So the leak is expected to be prevented by that runtime control. This depends on runtime behavior that could not be run here. The deciding fact that is not confirmed: that Node 22 drops Authorization on the cross-origin 307 to the tenant's shard host. If it does, this concern is refuted. The code itself has no safeguard (no redirect:'manual', no allowlist), so the protection relies entirely on the runtime.","impact":"If the runtime control were missing or bypassed (e.g., a same-origin redirect chain under the tenant's control), a tenant could capture the platform-wide ACME_TOKEN and access every tenant's Acme data."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/routes/integrations.mjs","line":25,"title":"Blind SSRF / internal probing through the followed partner redirect, with status returned to the tenant","verdict":"needs_validation","severity":"medium","reason":"Sync follows the partner's 307 to a host the tenant picks and returns {reachable, status} to the caller. Nothing restricts the destination: no redirect:'manual', no host allowlist, no private-IP block. The deciding fact that is not confirmed: whether the partner consoles accept internal or private HTTPS hosts (IP literals, internal DNS names) as shard hosts. The README only says 'any HTTPS host'.","impact":"A tenant member could make the server send HTTPS GET requests to internal hosts on the server's network. From the returned status or the 500 response they could map internal services."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/http.mjs","line":16,"title":"Malformed or null JSON body returns 500 instead of a 400 client error; body size is unbounded","verdict":"confirmed","severity":"low","reason":"JSON.parse throws on invalid JSON, and the catch at line 22 maps it to 500 'internal error'. A body of 'null' sets body = null, and then body.partner at integrations.mjs:17 throws a TypeError, which also becomes 500. The loop at line 15 concatenates the whole request body with no size limit. The body is only read after authentication, which limits who can send it.","impact":"Authenticated clients get misleading 500s for bad input, which makes the API contract unclear. An authenticated user can also send very large bodies to use up server memory."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/partners/acme.mjs","line":4,"title":"Partner fetches have no timeout","verdict":"confirmed","severity":"low","reason":"Neither checkAcmeAccount nor checkGlobexAccount passes an AbortSignal or timeout. The redirect target is controlled by the tenant, so it can respond slowly and hold each sync request open.","impact":"A tenant can tie up server connections and memory by syncing against a slow shard host they control, degrading service for other tenants."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/routes/integrations.mjs","line":23,"title":"Cross-tenant access through sync or list (IDOR)","verdict":"rejected","severity":"high","reason":"Sync uses ownIntegration(), which returns null unless item.tenant === user.tenant, so another tenant's integration gives 404. GET /integrations filters by user.tenant (line 14). POST sets tenant from the session (line 18), not from the body. The existing controls refute this concern; only DELETE lacks the control.","impact":"None: tenants cannot read or sync other tenants' integrations through these routes."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/routes/integrations.mjs","line":17,"title":"Prototype key or arbitrary partner name used to call a non-check function","verdict":"rejected","severity":"medium","reason":"Object.hasOwn(checks, body.partner) only accepts the own keys 'acme' and 'globex'. Values like '__proto__', 'constructor' or 'toString' fail, so checks[item.partner] at line 25 can only resolve to the two partner functions.","impact":"None: no arbitrary function call and no 500 caused by an unknown partner."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/partners/globex.mjs","line":4,"title":"Path or host injection through accountRef in the partner URL","verdict":"rejected","severity":"low","reason":"encodeURIComponent encodes '/', '?', '#', '%' and ':', so accountRef cannot change the host or add query or path segments. The URL is built against a fixed BASE. The remaining edge case is that accountRef '.' or '..' is normalized to /v1/accounts/ or /v1/. That only reaches the same trusted partner API, and only reachable/status is returned.","impact":"Negligible: at most an extra GET to the partner's own /v1/ path, and no data is returned to the tenant."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/routes/integrations.mjs","line":22,"title":"Non-admin members can trigger sync","verdict":"rejected","severity":"low","reason":"The README limits integration management to admins, but describes sync as a reachability check. It does not change data, and members may read their own tenant's data. Sync stays limited to the member's own tenant through ownIntegration.","impact":"None beyond the member's own tenant; this matches the stated requirements."}],"hardening":["Use ownIntegration(user, params.id) in DELETE before deleting, and add tenant-isolation tests for every route (list, create, sync, delete) using u1/u3 across t1/t2.","Do not let the shared credential follow tenant-controlled redirects: call fetch with redirect:'manual', read the 307 Location yourself, and either (a) request the shard host without the platform credential, or (b) only follow to an allowlisted partner-owned host set. Never send ACME_TOKEN or GLOBEX_API_KEY to a host the tenant chose.","Add a runtime regression test with a local partner stub that returns 307 to a second origin, and assert that neither authorization nor x-api-key arrives there.","Block private, loopback and link-local destinations for any redirect target that is followed, and add an AbortSignal.timeout() to partner fetches.","Return 400 for invalid or non-object JSON bodies, cap the request body size, and fail fast at startup if ACME_TOKEN or GLOBEX_API_KEY is unset (otherwise the literal string 'undefined' is sent).","Use non-sequential integration IDs (e.g., crypto.randomUUID()) to reduce enumeration."]}
