{"items":[{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/routes/billing.mjs","line":24,"title":"Refund total can exceed the charge amount because repeated refunds are allowed","verdict":"confirmed","severity":"high","reason":"README requires that 'A charge can be refunded at most once, for at most its amount.' The handler only checks `amount > charge.amount` against the original amount. It never checks `charge.refunded` (whether it is already non-zero, or what is left), and then does `charge.refunded += amount` (line 25). There is no other guard on this path. Two calls of POST /charges/c1/refund with {\"amount\":5000} both pass validation and both add a ledger entry, so refunded becomes 10000 on a 5000 charge. The tenant and admin checks (lines 20-22) are correct, but they do not stop this.","impact":"A tenant admin, or anyone holding an admin token, can refund the same charge without limit and pull out any multiple of the charged amount. The ledger records each refund, so money is actually paid out. Expected fix: reject the request when `charge.refunded > 0`, or at least when `charge.refunded + amount > charge.amount`. Then add tests for a second refund and for a cumulative over-refund."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/coupons.mjs","line":5,"title":"Coupon stays valid 9 hours past the end of its expiry day in Japan time","verdict":"confirmed","severity":"medium","reason":"README: `expiresOn` is valid 'through the end of that day in Japan time, and not after.' `new Date('2026-11-30')` uses the date-only ISO form, and the ECMAScript spec parses that form as UTC midnight no matter what TZ is set. So the cutoff is 2026-11-30T00:00Z + 24h = 2026-12-01T00:00Z, which is 2026-12-01 09:00 JST. The correct cutoff is 2026-12-01 00:00 JST (2026-11-30T15:00Z). Running the process with TZ=Asia/Tokyo does not change how date-only strings are parsed. The redeem route (billing.mjs:16) relies only on this function.","impact":"Members can redeem an expired coupon (for example the 20% WELCOME coupon) from 00:00 to 09:00 JST on the day after it expires, and get discounts the business did not intend. Expected fix: compare against `Date.parse(expiresOn + 'T00:00:00+09:00') + DAY_MS`, or an equivalent explicit +09:00 boundary. Add tests at 23:59:59 and 00:00:00 JST."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/docs.mjs","line":2,"title":"Docs preview host allowlist is a string-prefix check and accepts other hosts","verdict":"confirmed","severity":"medium","reason":"README limits the preview to public help pages from `https://docs.example.com`. The check is `url.startsWith('https://docs.example.com')` and does not parse the URL or compare `hostname`. So `https://docs.example.com.attacker.net/x` passes, and the server fetches a host the attacker chose. `redirect: 'error'` only blocks redirects. It does not limit which host is fetched first. The route (billing.mjs:33-39) is open to every authenticated member and returns up to 2000 characters of the response body whatever the status code is.","impact":"Any member can make the server send HTTPS requests to hosts the attacker picks and read the replies, which breaks the stated restriction. Whether this reaches internal services is covered in the next item. Expected fix: parse with `new URL()` and require `protocol === 'https:'`, `hostname === 'docs.example.com'`, the default port and no userinfo. Add tests for the suffix-host, userinfo and port variants."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/docs.mjs","line":3,"title":"SSRF into internal-only services through the docs preview bypass","verdict":"needs_validation","severity":"high","reason":"The service runs next to internal-only services (README). Because of the prefix bypass, an attacker can supply a name like `docs.example.com.attacker.net` and point its DNS at an internal IP. However, the scheme must be https and the fetch verifies TLS. So the request only succeeds if the internal target presents a certificate valid for the attacker-chosen hostname, or if certificate verification is weakened in the deployment (for example NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED or a custom CA). The `https://docs.example.com@internal/` userinfo form depends on Node's fetch rejecting URLs that contain credentials. I did not confirm that in this runtime. Decisive unconfirmed fact: whether any internal endpoint can be reached over HTTPS with verification passing (deployment TLS and DNS configuration), and how Node 22 fetch handles userinfo URLs. Separately, the timing and error difference between connection failure and a TLS error still acts as an internal port-scanning oracle, even though both return 400.","impact":"If this is reachable, any member could read responses from internal-only services, such as metadata or admin APIs. At minimum, members can probe internal hosts and ports by timing the responses."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/settings.mjs","line":9,"title":"Prototype pollution through the settings deep merge","verdict":"rejected","severity":"high","reason":"Concern: a body like `{\"__proto__\":{\"isAdmin\":true}}` sent to PATCH /settings. JSON.parse creates `__proto__` as an ordinary own property, and Object.keys returns it. But every merge target is a null-prototype object: the per-tenant root comes from `newSettings()` = `Object.create(null)`, and every nested plain object is rebuilt as `Object.create(null)` before merging (line 9) rather than stored by reference. A null-prototype object has no inherited `__proto__` setter, so `target['__proto__'] = ...` only creates an own data property, and `constructor`/`prototype` are not inherited either. Arrays and primitives are assigned directly but are never used as merge targets, because isPlainObject excludes arrays. Each tenant's document is chosen by `user.tenant` and the route is admin-only (billing.mjs:30-31). This matches the README's free-form JSON contract.","impact":"Object.prototype cannot be polluted and another tenant's settings cannot be reached. Only the caller's own tenant document changes, which is the intended behaviour."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/routes/billing.mjs","line":16,"title":"Cross-tenant access to coupons and charges","verdict":"rejected","severity":"high","reason":"Coupon redeem rejects the request when `coupon.tenant !== user.tenant`, with the same error as a missing coupon. Refund returns 404 when `charge.tenant !== user.tenant` and needs `role === 'admin'` (lines 20-22). Settings are taken from `settings.get(user.tenant)` and need admin. `user` always comes from the server-side session map (auth.mjs). It is never taken from the request body, and a Map lookup cannot be fooled with prototype keys. Unauthenticated requests are rejected before the body is read (http.mjs:12-13).","impact":"A member cannot read or change another tenant's coupons, charges or settings through these routes."},{"file":"<RUN>/target/src/http.mjs","line":15,"title":"Request body is read with no size limit, and malformed or non-object JSON returns 500","verdict":"confirmed","severity":"low","reason":"The body is concatenated with no size cap (line 15). Invalid JSON, or a valid non-object body such as `null`, makes JSON.parse throw or makes the handler's `body.code`/`body.url` access throw. Both fall into the generic 500 (line 22-23) rather than a 400. PATCH /settings with a string or array body merges index keys (`\"0\"`, `\"1\"`, ...) into the settings. Only authenticated users get as far as reading the body.","impact":"An authenticated user can use up memory with a very large body. Clients get 500 instead of a validation error, and tenant admins can store junk keys. No cross-tenant effect."}],"hardening":["Add tests for the README requirements, which currently have none. Only auth is tested. Needed: refund once and within the amount, the JST coupon boundary, tenant isolation for each route, the settings merge with __proto__/constructor keys, and docs-preview host validation.","Add a timeout (AbortSignal.timeout) and a response size limit to the docs preview fetch, and consider resolving the hostname and blocking private or link-local IPs as a second layer of defence.","Cap request body size and return 400 for invalid or non-object JSON bodies before routing.","Make each refund an explicit state transition (for example a `refundedAt` field or status) and an idempotent ledger entry, so 'at most once' is built into the data model rather than relying on an amount check.","Set a maximum nesting depth and size for the settings merge, so deep recursion from very nested JSON cannot cause stack exhaustion and 500s."]}
